// rzcobs_core — the rzCOBS encoder as a state machine that allocates
// nothing, holds no buffer, and builds for a microcontroller.
//
// Every body is a `todo()`.  The signatures are the design, and the
// compiler checks them as claims; see the README for the stamp.
//
// WHY THERE IS A SECOND MODULE.  `rzcobs` speaks `Bytes` and `Cursor`,
// and neither links at `@tier(embedded)`: the embedded runtime defines
// no `novo_bytes_*` symbol, so a device that called one would fail at
// the linker rather than at the type checker.  One host-only function
// anywhere in a compilation unit is an undefined symbol on a device
// whether or not the firmware calls it, so the half a device uses is
// its own module and takes and returns integers.
//
// WHY IT IS AN ENCODER AND NOT A CODEC.  rzCOBS is decoded from the
// END of a frame backwards, so a decoder needs the whole frame in one
// place before it can start.  A device sending logs never decodes one.
// The decoder is in `rzcobs`, over a buffer, where the host is.
//
// THE STATE IS TWO INTEGERS.  `run` counts the bytes of the current
// chunk that have been consumed; `zeros` is the 7-bit map of which of
// those were zero.  `zeros` is non-zero only while `run` is below 7,
// because a chunk that reaches 7 with a zero in it is flushed at once —
// so `run >= 7` is exactly "a run of non-zero bytes is open", and the
// two fields need no third flag to tell the modes apart.

// The most bytes of a chunk one bitmap header can cover.
//
// Seven, because a header byte below 0x80 is a bitmap with one bit per
// covered byte and the top bit is what distinguishes it from a run
// header.  It is the reason a decoded message can come back with up to
// six zero bytes appended: the last chunk is padded to seven.
pub fn bitmap_span() -> Int
    todo("rzcobs_core.bitmap_span")

// The most literal bytes one run header can cover.
//
// 134, which is the 0xFF header — `1nnnnnnn` covers `n + 7` literals
// and `n` is seven bits, so 127 + 7.  It is also the format's overhead
// bound: a message of non-zero bytes costs one header per 134 of them.
pub fn run_span() -> Int
    todo("rzcobs_core.run_span")

// The byte that ends a frame.
//
// Zero, and it is the one header value the encoding never produces —
// which is what lets a receiver split a stream into frames by looking
// for it and nothing else.
pub fn terminator() -> Int
    todo("rzcobs_core.terminator")

// The encoder between two bytes: the chunk it has open, and nothing
// else.
//
// A `@value` struct, so it lives in the caller's stack frame with no
// header, no reference count and no allocation.  An interrupt handler
// that owns one owns eight or sixteen bytes of its own frame.
//
// `run` is how many of the current chunk's bytes have been consumed —
// 0..6 while a bitmap is being built, 7..133 while a run of non-zero
// bytes is open.  `zeros` is the bitmap, bit `i` set when the `i`th
// byte of the chunk was zero; it is 0 whenever `run` is 7 or more.
pub @value
struct RzEncoder
    run: Int
    zeros: Int

// What one step of the encoder produced: the encoder after the step,
// and the bytes to write.
//
// `out` is a fixed two-byte inline array rather than a list because two
// is the most any step can produce — a literal and the header that
// closes the chunk it filled — and a list would be a heap allocation
// in an interrupt handler.  `len` is how many of the two are real,
// 0, 1 or 2; a step that emitted nothing leaves `len` at 0 and the
// caller writes nothing.
//
// This is the shape the whole no-allocation claim rests on.  A `@value`
// struct cannot be a `Result` payload, an optional payload or a field
// of a boxed struct, so an encoder that reports through one of those
// has left the tier it was written for.  Returning the new state and
// the bytes together in one unboxed value is the shape that stays.
pub @value
struct RzEmit
    enc: RzEncoder
    out: [u8; 2]
    len: Int

// A fresh encoder, with no chunk open.
//
// ```novo
// use rzcobs_core
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let e = rzcobs_core.encoder()
//     println("${e.run} ${e.zeros}")   // 0 0
// ```
pub fn encoder() -> RzEncoder
    todo("rzcobs_core.encoder")

// Feed one byte, and answer the encoder after it with the bytes to
// write.
//
// The whole format is in this function, and it is four cases:
//
//   * a chunk is being built (`run` under 7) and `b` is zero — set bit
//     `run` of the bitmap and emit nothing; the zero never goes on the
//     wire, which is the compression;
//   * a chunk is being built and `b` is not zero — emit `b` as a
//     literal;
//   * either of the above brought `run` to 7 with a non-empty bitmap —
//     also emit the bitmap as the header, and start a new chunk.  A
//     bitmap of 0 is not emitted, because 0x00 ends the frame: seven
//     non-zero bytes leave the run open instead, which is the third
//     case;
//   * a run of non-zero bytes is open (`run` at least 7).  A non-zero
//     `b` is one more literal, and at 134 of them the 0xFF header
//     closes the run with no trailing zero.  A zero `b` closes it with
//     `(run - 7) | 0x80`, and that header's own trailing zero is the
//     byte — so, again, the zero never goes on the wire.
//
// Every case emits at most two bytes, which is why `out` holds two.
//
// ```novo
// use rzcobs_core
//
// // Six literals and then a zero: the zero is not written, it is what
// // the 0x40 header stands for.
// fn main() [io]
//     var e = rzcobs_core.encoder()
//     var step = rzcobs_core.push(e, 0x11 as u8)
//     println("${step.len}")   // 1
// ```
pub fn push(e: RzEncoder, b: u8) -> RzEmit
    todo("rzcobs_core.push")

// Close the message and answer the last header and the terminator.
//
// A chunk that is still open is padded to seven with zeros and written
// as a bitmap; an open run is written as `(run - 7) | 0x80`; a chunk
// that consumed nothing writes no header at all.  The 0x00 terminator
// follows in every case, so `len` is 1 or 2 and never 0.
//
// The padding is why the round trip is not exact: a message of one
// non-zero byte comes back as that byte followed by six zeros, because
// the bitmap that carried it covers seven slots and the decoder has no
// way to know which of them the sender meant.  `defmt` lives with this
// — its frames are self-delimiting above this layer — and a caller who
// cannot must carry its own length.
//
// ```novo
// use rzcobs_core
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let step = rzcobs_core.finish(rzcobs_core.encoder())
//     println("${step.len}")   // 1
// ```
pub fn finish(e: RzEncoder) -> RzEmit
    todo("rzcobs_core.finish")

// The most bytes `push` and `finish` can produce for a message of
// `len` bytes, terminator included.
//
// `len + ceil(len / 134) + 1`.  One header per 134 non-zero bytes is
// the worst case, because a chunk with a zero in it spends its header
// on a byte it did not write; the `+ 1` is the terminator.  A caller
// sizing a transmit buffer wants this number and never a byte more.
//
// ```novo
// use rzcobs_core
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("${rzcobs_core.max_encoded_len(0)}")     // 1
//     println("${rzcobs_core.max_encoded_len(134)}")   // 136
// ```
pub fn max_encoded_len(len: Int) -> Int
    todo("rzcobs_core.max_encoded_len")
